# Forge 1.20.1 Mod Generator Application

This plan describes the creation of a standalone Java application with a graphical interface (Java Swing) that generates a complete, working Minecraft Forge 1.20.1 mod project.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The resulting application is going to be written in Java using Swing for the GUI. It will download the official Forge 1.20.1 MDK under the hood when generating a mod. You will need to build the Java application on your own using Maven, or I can run the package command for you. Please let me know if you are okay with downloading the Forge MDK dynamically, or if you prefer me to bundle a stripped-down template (which is harder to maintain). I recommend dynamic download to ensure an exact match with standard Forge tools.

## Proposed Changes

### Application Core & GUI

I will create a root Maven file to define the project (no external dependencies except standard Java).
The logic will be encapsulated in a single or a few Java class files to ensure simplicity.

#### [NEW] [pom.xml](file:///home/vladimir/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/minecraft_mod_maker/pom.xml)
A standard Maven file to manage the build of the standalone Mod Maker Java app.

#### [NEW] [ModMakerApp.java](file:///home/vladimir/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/minecraft_mod_maker/src/main/java/com/modmaker/ModMakerApp.java)
The main entry point and UI layout. Contains inputs for Mod ID, Mod Name, Block Name, a field and button to choose a texture, and an output directory selector. Includes a "Generate" button.

#### [NEW] [ModGenerator.java](file:///home/vladimir/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/minecraft_mod_maker/src/main/java/com/modmaker/ModGenerator.java)
Contains the core logic that:
1. Downloads the `forge-1.20.1-47.3.0-mdk.zip`.
2. Extracts it to the target directory.
3. Modifies `gradle.properties` based on user inputs.
4. Cleans up default template files (`com.example.examplemod`).
5. Generates the `Main` mod class with `DeferredRegister` for blocks/items.
6. Generates JSON files (blockstates, models, lang, etc.) for the new block.
7. Copies the user-selected block texture into the correct `assets` path.

## Open Questions

> [!WARNING]
> Do you want the mod generation process to use a specific base material for the block? By default, the plan uses `Blocks.STONE` properties for the newly generated block (meaning it will require a pickaxe, have stone sound, etc.).

## Verification Plan

### Automated Tests
1. I will run the Maven build (`mvn clean package`) for the Mod Maker Java App to verify it compiles.

### Manual Verification
1. I will ask you to run the `ModMakerApp.jar`.
2. You will generate a mod by entering the mod details, choosing an output folder, and selecting an image for the texture.
3. Then we will check the generated directory to verify it can run `./gradlew build` without issues.
